\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Effect of each change on the full system. Points are paired differences in options correct on TEST-A and TEST-B with 95\% video-bootstrap intervals; filled markers exclude zero. The extension past the video end and the hand-off timing matter most; the learned parts matter little, and removing the learned pair energy helps.}}{13}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:changes}{{4}{13}{Effect of each change on the full system. Points are paired differences in options correct on TEST-A and TEST-B with 95\% video-bootstrap intervals; filled markers exclude zero. The extension past the video end and the hand-off timing matter most; the learned parts matter little, and removing the learned pair energy helps}{figure.4}{}}
\newlabel{fig:changes@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][7][]13}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The evidence behind each answer. (a) Most correct answers rest on an event that can be checked against CLEVRER's annotation, and 98.2\% of those checks pass; the rest are decided in simulation, where no ground truth exists. (b) The deciding collision is found a median of 4 frames before the annotated contact. (c) Reported events against true events: recorded CLEVRER videos, simulated CoPhy counterfactuals with 2 to 6 balls, and simulated ComPhy futures.}}{14}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:evidence}{{5}{14}{The evidence behind each answer. (a) Most correct answers rest on an event that can be checked against CLEVRER's annotation, and 98.2\% of those checks pass; the rest are decided in simulation, where no ground truth exists. (b) The deciding collision is found a median of 4 frames before the annotated contact. (c) Reported events against true events: recorded CLEVRER videos, simulated CoPhy counterfactuals with 2 to 6 balls, and simulated ComPhy futures}{figure.5}{}}
\newlabel{fig:evidence@cref}{{[figure][5][]5}{[1][7][]14}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Two meanings of ``responsible''. (a) When CLEVRER's key marks an object responsible for a collision, its own counterfactual answers say the pair still collides without that object in 35.3\% of cases. (b) CLEVRER's chain rule (grey) matches CLEVRER's key; removing the cause and checking whether the collision still happens (blue) matches human judgments better.}}{14}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:responsible}{{6}{14}{Two meanings of ``responsible''. (a) When CLEVRER's key marks an object responsible for a collision, its own counterfactual answers say the pair still collides without that object in 35.3\% of cases. (b) CLEVRER's chain rule (grey) matches CLEVRER's key; removing the cause and checking whether the collision still happens (blue) matches human judgments better}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:responsible@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][8][]14}{}{}{}}
